REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0002"],"lenses":["4B.5","4B.8","4B.10"]}

New evidence
- `CONSTITUTION OF THE COMMUNIST PARTY OF CHINA(AMENDMENT)` gives the clearest primary-text statement of the Party's supremacy in 2022, including Xi-centered centralized leadership.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Full text of Constitution of Communist Party of China","publisher":"english.court.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-10-27","url":"https://english.court.gov.cn/2022-10/27/c_824962.htm","claim":"The amended CPC constitution says the Party is the leadership core, uses Xi Jinping Thought as a guide, and requires members to uphold Xi's core position and centralized, unified leadership.","locator":"2022-10-27, General Program, lines 133-163","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary party text published immediately after the 20th Congress; directly states formal hierarchy and ideological discipline.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target period.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Xi-led Party rule; this is the governing party's own constitutional language, not an external inference.","contrary_evidence":["This is self-description by the ruling party, not an independent democratic benchmark.","It shows asserted hierarchy, not public contestability."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.4","4B.5","4B.8","4B.10"]}
- `China's 20th Communist Party Congress: What you need to know` shows Xi's third term and a leadership team chosen for loyalty rather than pluralism.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China's 20th Communist Party Congress: What you need to know","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-10-20","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/10/20/china-congress-snapshot","claim":"Reuters reported that Xi secured a precedent-breaking third leadership term and introduced a new top governing body stacked with loyalists, while sidelining potential rivals.","locator":"Reuters body text, lines 655-676","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting on the congress leadership outcome and composition.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 congress event.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Xi's leadership consolidation at the party congress; it supports attribution of the entrenchment to Xi, though it does not prove personal selection of every appointee.","contrary_evidence":["The article reports a party process rather than a direct popular mandate.","Some staffing outcomes may reflect elite bargaining as well as Xi's preferences."],"lenses":["4B.1","4B.2","4B.5","4B.8","4B.10"]}
- `Hu Jintao escorted out of party congress` is strong evidence of stage-managed politics and censorship around the 2022 Party Congress.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Chinese ex-President Hu Jintao escorted out of party congress","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-10-23","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/10/23/china-congress-hujintao","claim":"Hu Jintao was unexpectedly escorted out of the congress, the incident was tightly stage-managed, and references to it were scrubbed from Weibo while state media omitted the scene.","locator":"Reuters body text, lines 658-678","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters eyewitness account plus contemporaneous observations of online and state-media censorship.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 Congress close.","ruler_attribution":"The source does not prove Xi personally ordered the escort, but it clearly reflects the Xi-era party-state's control over protocol and information.","contrary_evidence":["Hu's removal is not itself proof of Xi's direct command.","The incident could partly reflect Hu's condition or routine security handling."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.6","4B.8"]}
- Amnesty framed Xi's third term as a continuation of deeper repression, including censorship during politically sensitive moments and power concentration across party, military, and state.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China: Xi Jinping's continued tenure as leader a disaster for human rights","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-10-23","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/10/china-xi-jinpings-continued-tenure-as-leader-a-disaster-for-human-rights/","claim":"Amnesty said Xi's decade in power featured arbitrary detentions, a crackdown on expression and association, crimes against humanity against Muslims in Xinjiang, a dramatic escalation in Hong Kong, censorship during sensitive political periods, and growing pressure on VPNs; it also noted Xi's concentration of party, military, and state power and the removal of the two-term presidency limit in 2018.","locator":"Opening statement and background section","source_type":"ngo","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous advocacy statement with multiple specific claims that are independently corroborated by other sources in this dossier.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 leadership renewal and late-2022 censorship environment.","ruler_attribution":"Directly Xi-attributed; the source ties the pattern to his tenure and to formal power concentration under his leadership.","contrary_evidence":["This is advocacy framing, not a neutral government record.","Some claims combine long-run trends with 2022 events."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.5","4B.6","4B.8","4B.10"]}
- Amnesty's protest warning captures the state's anticipated and then observed response to the 2022 blank-paper protests.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"China: Government must not detain peaceful protesters as unprecedented demonstrations break out across the country","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-11-27","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/11/china-lockdown-protests-covid/","claim":"Amnesty said peaceful protesters were using blank paper and other creative dissent, and warned that authorities would likely answer with censorship, surveillance, police force, mass arrests, and long prison sentences rather than listen to grievances.","locator":"Lines 63-82","source_type":"ngo","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous rights statement that is directly matched by later Reuters reporting on police follow-up and censorship.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact late-2022 protest wave.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Xi-led Chinese government and its security apparatus.","contrary_evidence":["The statement says Amnesty could not independently verify every online video.","It is a forward-looking warning rather than a full post-event accounting."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- Reuters gives the clearest contemporaneous account of how protesters used blank sheets to evade censorship and how thoroughly dissent had been squeezed out.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Blank sheets of paper become symbol of defiance in China protests","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-11-27","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/coronavirus/blank-sheets-of-paper-become-symbol-of-defiance-in-china-protests-2952307","claim":"Reuters reported that protesters used blank sheets of paper to evade censorship or arrest, described the protests as a rare widespread outpouring of dissent, and said room for dissent had been all but eliminated under Xi.","locator":"Lines 432-453","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters contemporaneous reporting with multiple observations from street protests and university campuses.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 protest wave.","ruler_attribution":"Directly describes the Xi-era political climate; the article does not claim Xi personally ordered each police act.","contrary_evidence":["Some video material was not independently verified.","The article focuses on the protest climate, not the legal aftercare."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- Reuters also documented direct police follow-up, which is useful for the surveillance and harassment lenses.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Chinese authorities seek out COVID protesters","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-11-28","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/coronavirus/china-police-out-in-numbers-to-prevent-more-covid-protests-2953228","claim":"Reuters reported that Chinese authorities began inquiries into weekend protesters, police remained out in numbers, and people were asked to provide written accounts while officers checked phones and messaging apps to identify participants.","locator":"Opening paragraphs and mid-article reporting on Beijing and Shanghai policing","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct Reuters field reporting on immediate police response to protests.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 protest aftercare.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to Chinese authorities under Xi's leadership; the source does not establish a single personal command from Xi.","contrary_evidence":["Some actions were done by local police and colleges rather than the central leadership directly.","The article does not prove all questioning led to arrests."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}
- The State Council's December policy shift is the main favorable/countervailing item in the file, showing partial retreat from the harshest pandemic controls.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"COVID-19 response further optimized with 10 new measures","publisher":"english.www.gov.cn / Xinhua","publication_date":"2022-12-08","url":"https://english.www.gov.cn/statecouncil/ministries/202212/08/content_WS63913c92c6d0a757729e4135.html","claim":"China released 10 new COVID-response measures telling localities to rectify one-size-fits-all excesses, prohibit arbitrary expansion of high-risk areas, and move to more targeted control.","locator":"Headline and opening summary","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official policy statement, contemporaneous to the late-2022 protest wave, showing a real though limited course correction.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 policy reversal after protests.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Xi-led state apparatus rather than Xi personally; it reflects central policy direction.","contrary_evidence":["The measures kept the regime restrictive, even if less blanket than before.","They came after months of coercive lockdowns and public protest."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- OHCHR's Xinjiang assessment is the strongest independent cross-check on minority rights and political equality.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"OHCHR Assessment of human rights concerns in the Xinjiang Uyghur Autonomous Region, People's Republic of China","publisher":"UN Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights","publication_date":"2022-08-31","url":"https://www.ohchr.org/sites/default/files/documents/countries/2022-08-31/22-08-31-final-assesment.pdf","claim":"OHCHR concluded that serious human rights violations occurred in Xinjiang against Uyghurs and other predominantly Muslim communities, with severe and undue restrictions on rights and discriminatory detention patterns tied to China's counter-terror and counter-extremism policies.","locator":"Assessment summary / conclusion","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN rights office report based on documentary review, government materials, and applicable international law.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 report on the target-year environment.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Xi-led Chinese government; the report explicitly assessed government laws, policies, data, and statements.","contrary_evidence":["China rejects the assessment and frames the policy as counter-terrorism.","The report is an assessment, not a court judgment."],"lenses":["4B.7","4B.10"]}
- HRW's Hong Kong section captures press, protest, university, and surveillance repression under Beijing's national-security framework.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: China","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023-01","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/china","claim":"HRW reported that Hong Kong authorities attacked press freedom, censored sensitive material, intensified surveillance, and obstructed student unions and universities under the Beijing-imposed national-security framework.","locator":"Hong Kong section, lines 89-101","source_type":"ngo","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed annual country report from a major human-rights monitor, written from the 2022 event record.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"2022 events summarized in a 2023 annual report.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Beijing under Xi's consolidated control, though carried out by Hong Kong authorities and institutions.","contrary_evidence":["Some implementation was by Hong Kong officials rather than direct Beijing agents.","The section summarizes multiple incidents rather than one discrete act."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.4","4B.6","4B.8","4B.9","4B.10"]}
- CPJ's annual press census adds an independent media-freedom cross-check on China as a surveillance-heavy jailer of journalists.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Attacks on the Press in 2022","publisher":"Committee to Protect Journalists","publication_date":"2022-12","url":"https://cpj.org/2022/12/attacks-on-the-press-in-2022/","claim":"CPJ said China ranked #2 among jailers of journalists in 2022, with 43 known journalists jailed, and warned that the country's tight media censorship and pervasive surveillance meant the figure should not be read as any easing of intolerance for independent reporting.","locator":"China section, lines 597-599","source_type":"ngo","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual CPJ prison census is a strong specialized source, though it is a snapshot and understates hidden cases.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 annual snapshot.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Xi-era censorship and surveillance systems; CPJ does not attribute each imprisonment to Xi personally.","contrary_evidence":["The count is a snapshot and likely understates concealed detention.","It does not by itself separate central from local responsibility."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9"]}

Search and rejection summary
- I considered roughly 30 documents across Reuters syndications, official CPC/State Council texts, Amnesty, HRW, OHCHR, CPJ, and RSF, plus a few duplicated wire copies and multilingual reposts.
- I retained the smallest set that gave me the 2022 power-consolidation record, the protest/crackdown record, the Xinjiang/minority-rights record, the Hong Kong media/surveillance record, and one favorable policy-relaxation counterexample.
- I rejected duplicate syndications of the same Reuters story across Euronews, Investing, Geo, TBS, ThePrint, and other mirrors when the underlying claim was identical.
- I rejected out-of-period or low-yield material, including 2026 follow-ups and older background pieces that did not add a materially different 2022 claim.
- I did not rely on the local structured datasets again for Freedom House, V-Dem, WGI, Polity, or similar baseline indices, because the local prior already supplied the country-year context.
- I rejected broad opinion or analysis pieces that only repeated the same facts without adding a distinct claim or a clearer attribution chain.

Remaining gaps
- There is still no direct source proving Xi personally ordered each protest arrest, the Hu Jintao escort, or the exact mechanics of every censorship action. Attribution is strongest to the Xi-led party-state apparatus, not to a single signed order.
- The record on 4B.4 remains mostly negative rather than affirmative: I found no strong 2022 evidence that Xi strengthened independent courts, legislatures, audit bodies, or other oversight institutions.
- The national-election question is structurally simple in China because meaningful contestability was already absent; the best evidence here is the 2022 congress and party-constitution language, not an election event.
- More searching could still help on formal appointment records, internal party discipline documents, or Hong Kong court/legislative records, but it is unlikely to change the broad 2022 picture: further centralization, sharper censorship, and only a narrow late-year policy retreat on COVID controls.
